<main class="page">
  <div class="content">
    <a href="/blog" class="back">← Back to Blog</a>
    
    <h1>Pulse v0.6.0: The Beginning</h1>
    <span class="date">January 15, 2025</span>
    
    <div class="body">
      <p class="lead">Pulse was born out of a desire for a web framework that leverages the raw performance of modern runtimes while keeping the developer experience simple. v0.6 is our first public preview.</p>
      
      <h3>⚡ Built on Bun</h3>
      <p>Pulse relies entirely on the <strong>Bun</strong> runtime. By skipping the Node.js legacy compatibility layer, we achieve build times that are measured in milliseconds, not seconds. The bundler, dev server, and test runner are all unified into a single native binary workflow.</p>

      <h3>📡 Signal-Based Reactivity</h3>
      <p>At the heart of Pulse is a fine-grained reactivity system based on Signals. Similar to SolidJS, our signals track their own dependencies. This means components only re-run when their specific data changes. There is no Virtual DOM diffing overhead.</p>

      <div class="code-block">
        <code>const [count, setCount] = createSignal(0);</code>
      </div>

      <h3>📄 File Structure</h3>
      <p>We introduced the <code>.pulse</code> file format. It combines HTML, logic, and scoped CSS into a single cohesive unit, inspired by Svelte and Vue, but stripped down to the bare essentials needed for high performance.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</main>

